  {
    "id": "fse-project/fse/README.md",
    "name": "README.md",
    "path": "fse-project/fse/README.md",
    "extension": ".md",
    "isFolder": false,
    "size": 3404,
    "modifiedAt": "2026-10-30T10:36:20.493Z",
    "content": "# DSA File Search Engine (C)\n\nA command-line file manager and search engine built to show where each data structure earns its keep.\nWorks on a **sandbox root folder** – every operation is confined to it (`..`, absolute paths and drive letters are rejected).\nBuilds on Windows (MinGW-w64) and Linux/macOS from the same source (`platform.h` + `util.c` hold the OS-specific parts).\n\n## Build & run\n\n### Windows (VS Code + MinGW-w64, no admin rights or WSL needed)\n1. Get a compiler: download a **WinLibs** GCC zip (winlibs.com, \"UCRT runtime\", Win64), extract it to `C:\\mingw64`,\n   and add `C:\\mingw64\\bin` to your **user** PATH (Start > \"Edit environment variables for your account\").\n2. Install VS Code + the **C/C++** extension (Microsoft).\n3. `File > Open Folder...` and choose this project folder.\n4. Build: **Ctrl+Shift+B** (or run `build.bat` in the terminal). Run in the VS Code terminal:\n   `.\\fse.exe sandbox`  (then type `demo`, `help`)\n5. Debug: set a breakpoint, press **F5**.\n`prebuilt\\fse.exe` is a ready-made 64-bit build if you just want to try it.\n\n### Linux / macOS / WSL\n```\nmake\n./fse ./sandbox\nmake debug      # AddressSanitizer + UBSan build\n```\nOne-shot mode works everywhere: `fse <root> <command> [args]`\n\n## Where each data structure is used\n| Structure | File | Used for | Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Linked list | `filelist.c` | master list of all `FileRecord`s; `filter` and `stats` traverse it | append O(1), scan O(n) |\n| Hash table (chaining, auto-resize) | `hashtable.c` | `find <exact_name>` | O(1) average |\n| Trie (sorted sibling lists) | `trie.c` | `search <prefix>`, case-insensitive | O(len + matches) |\n| Queue | `queue.c` | BFS over folders while scanning; BFS over the relationship graph | O(1) push/pop |\n| General tree (first-child/next-sibling) | `tree.c` | folder hierarchy: `tree`, `ls`, recursive folder sizes, path lookup | O(depth × siblings) |\n| Graph (adjacency lists) | `graph.c` | `related <path>`: edges = identical content / same filename | BFS O(V+E) |\n| Hashing + sorting | `dupes.c` | `dups`: group by size → FNV-1a content hash only on size collisions → byte-compare | reads few files |\n\n## Commands\n```\nls [path]  tree [path] [depth]  scan  pwd\nfind <exact_name>               hash table lookup\nsearch <prefix>                 trie prefix search\nfilter ext= type=file|dir min= max= newer= older= contains= sort=name|size|date order= limit=\ndups                            duplicate files by content\nrelated <path>                  graph + BFS of linked files\nstats                           storage dashboard\nmkdir <path>  touch <path> [text]  rename|mv <path> <new>  rm [-r] [-f] <path>\ndemo  help  exit\n```\nExamples: `filter ext=c min=1k sort=size` · `filter type=file newer=7` · `filter older=365 limit=10`\n\n## Design notes\n- **Index rebuild after mutations**: `mkdir/touch/rename/rm` change the disk, then call `engine_rebuild()` so all five\n  structures stay consistent. Simple and always correct (~160 ms for 20k entries). \n- **Lazy work**: content hashing, duplicate groups and the graph are built only when first needed, then cached until the next change.\n- Symlinks and special files are skipped.\n\n## Ideas for extension\nIncremental index updates (remove/insert in hash+trie instead of rebuild), `grep`-style content search,\nfuzzy matching with edit distance on the trie, export dashboard to CSV, persisting the index to disk.\n"
  },
